FFmpeg — LGPL-2.1-or-later
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
This software links against FFmpeg (avformat, avcodec, avutil, swresample)
and is not itself a work of FFmpeg. The binaries distributed by this project
use FFmpeg under the LGPL: their generated config.h reports CONFIG_GPL 0 and
CONFIG_NONFREE 0. The build scripts also permit explicit custom GPL or nonfree
builds; those builds have different redistribution terms and are not covered
by the LGPL distribution guidance below.

Full license text: https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html
Source: https://ffmpeg.org/download.html

Every binary this project distributes builds FFmpeg from source: the
unmodified release tarball https://ffmpeg.org/releases/ffmpeg-<version>.tar.xz,
where <version> is ffaudio_ffmpeg_version in native/codec-set.sh, configured
with the component list in that same file (selected by FFAUDIO_VARIANT) and
the flags in the platform's script below. That tarball and those scripts, at
the tag of the release that shipped the binary, are its complete
corresponding source.

How FFmpeg is linked, and how an end user replaces it:

  Windows. FFmpeg is a set of separate DLLs (avformat-*.dll, avcodec-*.dll,
  avutil-*.dll, swresample-*.dll) beside ffaudio.dll, built shared with
  MSVC by native/windows/build-ffmpeg.sh, which native/windows/build.ps1
  runs. Replacing them with an ABI-compatible build is enough.

  macOS, Linux, iOS and Android. FFmpeg is linked statically into the
  façade (libffaudio.dylib, libffaudio.so, ffaudio.framework), which is
  itself a separate shared library, so replaceability is met by relinking
  it rather than by FFmpeg being dynamic: native/host-ffmpeg.sh (run by
  native/macos/build.sh and native/linux/build.sh under --static),
  native/ios/build-ffmpeg.sh and native/android/build-ffmpeg.sh configure
  FFmpeg, and the corresponding build.sh links the façade against it.
  Rebuilding the façade from an FFmpeg of the user's own choosing is those
  scripts with --ffmpeg-version or --configure-flags (or, on macOS and
  Linux, without --static and with pkg-config pointed at their own FFmpeg),
  and dropping the result in place of the shipped library.

  The development builds of macOS and Linux (without --static) link
  whatever FFmpeg pkg-config finds and are not distributed.

  A binary can also be asked directly, which is what a person holding one
  actually has: ffaudio_ffmpeg_configuration returns the configure line
  FFmpeg itself recorded, and ffaudio_ffmpeg_license returns avutil's own
  answer about the terms. FFmpegBuild exposes both to managed callers. That
  is the authoritative statement about a given artifact; the scripts describe
  how it was meant to be built.

